# Fontly build rules

## What this project is

Fontly is a static font generator website. Visitors type text, the tool
renders it in many Unicode styles, and they copy a style to paste into
Instagram, TikTok, Discord or WhatsApp. Every page carries a long article
below the tool. The tool is the hero and sits above the fold on every page.

The owner is a beginner. When you make a decision that was not specified,
say so in one line and say why. If something asked for is technically wrong
or would hurt speed, say so instead of quietly doing it. If a phase is not
finished, say it is not finished. Never report work as done that you have
not verified.

## Priorities, in this exact order

1. Page speed. This site competes against slow, ad-heavy pages. Speed is
   the product. If a feature costs speed, the feature loses.
2. Correctness. Every character the tool outputs must be the right Unicode
   code point. One wrong character makes the whole site look amateur.
3. Content manageability. Editing an article must never mean touching code.
4. Accessibility and honesty. Real buttons, real focus states, no fake
   claims anywhere in the interface.
5. Variety of styles. Last, deliberately. Forty correct styles beat three
   hundred sloppy ones.

## Hard constraints

Breaking any of these means the work has to be redone.

- No React, Vue, Svelte or Preact. Not even as an Astro integration. Zero
  UI framework JavaScript reaches the browser.
- No Tailwind, Bootstrap or any CSS framework. Hand-written CSS only.
- No web fonts. Not Google Fonts, not self-hosted, not one. System font
  stack only. This is about correctness, not performance: the whole site is
  about rendering unusual characters in the reader's own system font, and a
  web font would hide the exact rendering failures the articles document.
- No jQuery, no lodash, no UI libraries.
- No third-party script except Cloudflare Web Analytics, added last and
  deferred.
- No server, no database, no API routes. Output is pure static HTML, CSS
  and JS.
- No ads, no ad slots, no cookie banner, no popup, no newsletter modal.
- No embedded video, no social widget, no comment system.
- Do not add any library, framework or dependency without asking first.

## Speed budget

Non-negotiable. Report the real measured number after each phase,
including the ones that fail. Every phase report must state the measured
CLS and INP numbers, or state plainly that they were not measured and why.

- JavaScript: under 15 KB gzipped for the whole tool
- CSS: under 15 KB gzipped, critical CSS inlined
- Zero web font requests
- Zero third-party requests on first load
- LCP element is server-rendered text or cards, never an image
- CLS exactly 0
- INP under 200 ms while typing, measured at 4x CPU throttling
- Roughly 1,500 DOM nodes per page or fewer

Field data is what ranks, not Lighthouse. Google uses real Chrome visits
over a rolling 28-day window at the 75th percentile. Do not chase a
perfect lab score.

## Stack

- Astro, latest stable, static output, no UI framework integration
- Astro content collections with Markdown for all content
- Vanilla JavaScript for the generator, one module, loaded deferred
- Plain hand-written CSS
- System font stack only
- @astrojs/sitemap
- Cloudflare Web Analytics as the single script tag, at the very end,
  deferred
- Deployed to Cloudflare Pages from a GitHub repo. Host build command
  npx astro build, output directory dist. Cloudflare does not put
  node_modules/.bin on the shell path, so bare `astro build` fails with
  exit code 127. Proved on a real failed build on 21 August 2026. Do not
  install a Cloudflare adapter: the build is fully static and an adapter
  is only needed for server-rendered output. The owner pushes from
  Cursor's Source Control panel.

## Honesty rules

- Never invent a number, date, percentage or capability. If a fact belongs
  in the interface and it was not given to you, ask or leave a TODO.
- Never hardcode a style count anywhere. Render it from styles.length.
- These exact claims are banned because an earlier prototype shipped them
  and they are false: "Works Everywhere", any device-compatibility
  percentage such as "99%", and any hardcoded count such as "130+ styles".
- Never hardcode a compatibility claim of any kind.
- No statistic appears in the interface unless the owner supplies it.
- Only document features that exist and have been tested. Do not list
  keyboard shortcuts, or anything else, that has not been verified working.

## Accessibility

- Every interactive element is a real button or anchor, never a clickable
  div.
- Visible focus outlines. Do not remove them.
- Body text contrast at least 4.5 to 1.
- The whole tool works with a keyboard alone, in a sensible tab order.
- Copy confirmation is announced in an aria-live="polite" region, so screen
  reader users hear it and not only sighted users.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion on any transition.
- FAQ answers are present in the HTML source even when visually collapsed.

## SEO

- One h1 per page, taken from frontmatter.
- Canonical URL on every real page. Site is https://fontly.cool. Trailing
  slashes consistent everywhere.
- The 404 page carries no canonical and does carry a noindex robots directive.
  An error document is served at every broken URL a visitor types; a canonical
  on it would claim those URLs are one indexable page, and
  https://fontly.cool/404/ is not a real route this site generates.
- Add BreadcrumbList structured data on sub-pages. Omit it on the homepage,
  where a single self-pointing crumb describes no real position. It is the only
  structured data on this site that still changes what a searcher sees.
- Keep FAQPage and SoftwareApplication JSON-LD for machine understanding
  only. Do not claim they produce a visible search feature. FAQ rich
  results stopped appearing for every site on 7 May 2026, and HowTo was
  deprecated on desktop on 13 September 2023.
- @astrojs/sitemap generating /sitemap-index.xml, with robots.txt in
  public/ pointing to it.
- A real 404 page linking back to the homepage and the live tool pages.
- Semantic HTML. Headings in order, no skipped levels.
- No tag pages, no category archives, no date archives, no pagination.
- Internal linking is hub and spoke, three to five genuinely related links
  per page. Never link every page to every other page.

## Folder structure

- `src/content.config.ts`. Astro 7 moved this file out of the content folder.
  Beside it sit `src/content/pages/` and `src/content/blog/`
- src/data/styles.ts, src/data/categories.ts, src/data/pages.ts
- src/layouts/PageLayout.astro
- src/components/Tool.astro, Faq.astro, RelatedTools.astro, Schema.astro,
  Previews.astro, Header.astro, Footer.astro
- src/scripts/generator.js
- src/scripts/tool.js
- src/styles/global.css
- src/pages/[...slug].astro and src/pages/404.astro
- public/robots.txt

Adding a new page must mean adding one Markdown file and nothing else.

The filename is the URL. Astro derives an entry's id from its filename and the
route uses that id, so `bubble-text-generator.md` serves
`/bubble-text-generator/`. Name every file exactly for the slug fixed in the
keyword map, never a shortened version of it. There is no `slug` frontmatter
field on this site: Astro reserves that word, and a field Astro silently ignores
is exactly the kind of invisible trap these rules exist to prevent. The single
exception is the homepage, `index.md`, which the catch-all route maps to the site
root rather than to `/index/`.

## Layout and visual design

These rules come from the Notion design document. Agents cannot read Notion, so
they live here. Changing them without an explicit owner decision is a bug.

- Reading width is 680 to 720 px. Satisfied by 43.75rem (680px at a 16px root);
  rem so it respects a raised browser default font size. Do not re-choose it.
- The tool section is capped at 72rem. The earlier 52rem cap was a mistake; the
  design document says the tool runs full width within that wider measure. Do
  not re-choose it from scratch later. The tool remains the only element wider
  than the article spine.
- The h1 sits inside the article measure. Only the tool section uses the wider
  cap. One left edge for all prose (h1, article body, FAQ, related tools). Do
  not move the heading to match the tool edge; the tool gets a visible boundary
  instead so it reads as one object.
- One accent colour only, as a single CSS custom property (`--accent`). Edit
  that one line to retheme. Do not repeat the hex literal elsewhere in the
  stylesheet. Links, buttons, and the copy-button state use it. Nothing else
  on the site is coloured. Once chosen, every future page reuses the same
  values with no per-page restyling.
- System sans-serif only, with generous line-height for body copy next to dense
  Unicode examples. Zero web fonts — not Google Fonts, not self-hosted, not
  one `@font-face`. Hard constraint for correctness: a web font would hide the
  exact rendering failures the articles document.
- A table of contents is banned on every page, permanently.
- The mini sticky tool bar is specified in the design document but deferred on
  scope. Do not build it in an early phase.
- The visually distinct callout and tip box is specified in the design document
  but deferred until articles are converted, because there is no real tip
  content to put in it yet. When built, tips render as callout boxes.
- Site header: `Header.astro` in normal document flow (not sticky), rendered
  immediately above `<main>`. Entire content is the single word Fontly as a
  link to `/`. No navigation links of any kind until more than one page is
  live — links to pages that do not exist are banned. Semantic `<header>`.
  Full-width background with a bottom border is fine; the inner content must
  use the same 43.75rem measure as the article spine (h1, hero, prose, FAQ,
  related tools) so those elements share one left edge. The tool at 72rem is
  the only element wider than that spine.
- Site footer: `Footer.astro` in normal document flow, immediately below
  `<main>`. Entire content is three things only: the word Fontly, the address
  contact@fontly.cool as a mailto: link, and the line © 2026 Fontly. No
  tagline, nav list, social links, or legal page. Same 43.75rem inner measure
  as the article spine, semantic `<footer>`.
- No count may appear in visible prose until `styles.length` actually reaches
  it. That is why "200+" was deleted from the owner's hero intro line and must
  be restored only at launch. Title tags and meta descriptions that still
  carry an untrue count are temporary owner copy, not a licence to invent new
  counts in the interface.
- Tool style card layout: a header row with the style name, an optional caveat
  badge, and two icon buttons aligned right (favourite and copy); then the
  styled sample; then one compact line for the unit count. Quiet digit and case
  notes may sit under that line. Any icon-only control must carry an accessible
  name that includes the style name. No element may appear after first paint
  without its space reserved — copy confirmation uses a fixed icon-button box;
  digit notes use visibility, not display:none, when quiet.
- Tool card grid breakpoints on `.tool__cards`: one column below 40rem (640px
  at a 16px root), two columns from 40rem to 60rem (640px to 960px), three
  columns above 60rem (960px). No fixed column count outside these queries.
- This tool receives long strings with no spaces as normal input (usernames,
  gaming tags, Discord names). Any container that holds user text or generated
  output must wrap unbroken tokens (`overflow-wrap: anywhere` on `.article` and
  `.tool-card__output`). A grid column must never be sized by its own content —
  use `minmax(0, 1fr)`, not bare `1fr`. Found on a real phone: a fifty-four
  character sentinel with no spaces made the homepage body occupy about 69% of
  a 702px-wide screenshot (~485px), so the browser scaled the whole page down
  and 16px body text arrived at around 11px. `initial-scale=1` alone only swaps
  that for sideways scroll; wrapping the overflow is the fix.

## Build order. Stop after each phase and wait for review.

1. Astro project, folder structure, content collection config with Zod
   validation, layout, global CSS with the system font stack, and one
   Markdown page rendering end to end with a placeholder where the tool
   goes. No tool logic at all in this phase.
2. Style data and the generator function in isolation. Print every
   character of every style to the console. Nothing in the browser yet.
3. The tool component and the browser script. Report gzipped JS size.
4. FAQ, related tools, schema, 404, sitemap, robots.txt, canonicals.
5. A second page and then a third page added from Markdown only, to prove
   the structure holds without touching any other file.
6. Cloudflare Pages deploy config and the analytics snippet. Then report
   every acceptance number honestly, including the ones that fail.

Do not skip ahead. Do not build a later phase because it seems easy.
